# Aldermark Certification A/S — Food Safety Management System Audit Report

**Client:** Korvane Cold Chain Sp. z o.o., ul. Łużycka 14, 81-537 Gdynia, Poland
**Standard:** ISO 22000:2018 · **Certificate no.:** FSMS-PL-7741-2021 · **Report reference:** ACA/2024/PL/0319
**Audit type:** Surveillance audit no. 2 (SA2) of the three-year certification cycle · **Report issued:** 24 September 2024 · Page 1 of 5 (header repeated on each page, please disregard if it appears twice)

## 1. Purpose, scope and criteria

This report documents the outcome of the second surveillance audit of the food safety management system (FSMS) operated by Korvane Cold Chain Sp. z o.o. ("Korvane", "the organisation"). The purpose of a surveillance audit under Aldermark's certification rules is to verify, on a sampling basis, that the certified system continues to meet the requirements of ISO 22000:2018 and the organisation's own documented procedures, that the commitments given at the previous audit have been honoured, and that the system is still capable of delivering its intended results. It is not a full reassessment of the system; that is the task of the recertification audit, which is dealt with in section 8 of this report. For completeness we record that the first surveillance audit (SA1) was held in September 2023, the 2022 surveillance having been converted into a desk review under the transitional derogation then in force, and that the four minor nonconformities raised at SA1 were found to have been closed with effective evidence.

The certified scope reads: *"Reception, monitored storage, pre-trip inspection, plug-in supervision, cross-dock transfer and dispatch of temperature-controlled foodstuffs in refrigerated ISO containers, including the continuous monitoring of the cold chain by means of KC-Tag 4 data loggers, KC-Hub 200 yard gateways and the Tidewalk web application."* No change to the scope was requested by the client and none is proposed by the audit team. The audit criteria were ISO 22000:2018, the Korvane FSMS manual (document KC-QF-MAN-001, edition 7), the HACCP plan for reefer handling, applicable customer requirements, and Polish and EU food hygiene legislation as far as it bears on logistics operators.

The certificate covers three sites: Gdynia (GDY-1), Hamburg-Waltershof (HAM-2) and Rotterdam-Maasvlakte (RTM-3). Gothenburg-Arendal (GOT-4) and the trans-shipment yard at Kłaipėda (KLJ-5) are not certified sites, although the client has told us it intends to propose them for inclusion at the recertification. On the audit dates the organisation declared 297 employees within the scope of the certificate. The three on-site days were spent at GDY-1 and HAM-2 only; RTM-3 was sampled remotely, and GOT-4 and KLJ-5 were looked at briefly in the same videoconference as a courtesy and for planning purposes, with no conclusions drawn.

