| O-03 | 6.3 | Observation | KC-Hub 200 firmware release 3.8 was rolled out to HAM-2 and RTM-3 without a change record | n/a |

The due dates above were agreed with the client at the closing meeting. They are shorter than the 90-day maximum that Aldermark's rules grant for minor nonconformities (which would have run to 10 December 2024) because the recertification audit starting on 14 October 2024 falls inside that window and the audit team would like to see the corrections closed before it begins.

## 5. Nonconformity descriptions

**F-01 (major) — clause 8.9.4.2, evaluation of potentially unsafe product.** On 14 June 2024 a refrigerated container carrying dairy product for Oakhaven Dairy Ltd experienced a temperature excursion lasting 9 hours on the yard plug row at GDY-1. The audit team reviewed the disposition file. The load was held under a red hold label from 02:15 on 15 June but the release for onward delivery was recorded on form KC-QF-114 at 04:40 on 16 June, a hold of 26 hours 25 minutes, and was signed by the duty shift lead alone. The Food Safety Team Leader, who under section 6.4 of the organisation's own excursion procedure should evaluate every excursion exceeding 4 hours, was not called, because the night shift ("the owls") had no on-call number for that role and the authority matrix in the procedure named the shift lead as sufficient for "customer-released" loads. The consignee had in fact accepted the product on the strength of a verbal assurance. The auditors saw no evidence that a risk evaluation of the temperature–time profile was performed, and form KC-QF-114 in its revision B has no field for the evaluator's name. The audit team considers this a failure of the system, not of the individuals, and has graded it major under clause 8.9.4.2 (evaluation for release) with a cross-reference to 8.9.2 (corrections). The client has already opened corrective action request KC-CAR-24-088 on 17 September 2024 in response; the receipt of that request is acknowledged here, though its adequacy will be judged only when the plan has been formally submitted by the date in the findings table.

**F-02 (minor) — clause 7.1.5.1, monitoring and measuring resources.** At HAM-2 the audit team observed the reference thermometer RT-HAM-07 being used on 10 September 2024 to verify a KC-Tag 4 probe during a pre-trip inspection. The calibration certificate for the instrument (laboratory certificate series LK-2023) showed a validity that ended on 2 August 2024. The organisation's procedure sets a calibration interval of 12 months. The instrument should have been quarantined. The records of the previous 39 days of use were requested on the day and were not produced until the following morning, which the team notes as the sole instance of slow response during the audit. The expired certificate does not by itself invalidate the earlier readings; the deviation in the earlier certificate was 0.12 °C, well inside the tolerance.

**F-03 (minor) — clause 7.2, competence.** Of 41 reefer plug-in operators employed at HAM-2 on 10 September 2024, six had a HACCP awareness course (code HA-101) that had lapsed against the 24-month validity set in the training matrix. Two of the six had been on shift at the time of the audit. The organisation's training records system flagged the lapses in an overdue list, but no scheduled sessions had been booked.

**F-04 (minor) — clause 8.2.4, control of pests.** The pest control bait station map posted at GDY-1 gate 3 and the map held by the pest control contractor showed 46 stations. The audit team counted 49 in the field when walking the perimeter on 9 September 2024. The three additional stations were installed after the 2023 resurfacing of pad D and were serviced, but were never recorded on the map and so never appear in trend reports. No pest activity was evident.

**F-05 (minor) — clause 9.2.2, internal audit programme.** The internal audit programme for 2024 planned 14 audits in the first half-year. Two of them (the ones covering goods-in at RTM-3 and calibration at GOT-4) were carried out more than 60 days after the planned date, and the programme contains no record of why they were moved or who approved the move.

## 6. Positive notes

The audit team would like to record several strengths. The pre-trip inspection records sampled remotely at RTM-3 were complete in 40 out of 40 files selected, and Hanneke Dijkgraaf's team was able to retrieve any file within about two minutes. The night shift dispatchers interviewed at GDY-1, including Olek Maraszek, described the excursion escalation tiers accurately and without prompting. The Tidewalk alert history for the first eight months of 2024 gave a clear and searchable record of excursions. The management review of 21 March 2024 was properly minuted, with decisions, owners and dates. The client's commitment to disclosing the June 2024 excursion to the certification body promptly, even though it was embarrassing, was exemplary.

## 7. Observations (not graded)

**O-01** — The mock recall carried out on 12 March 2024 traced the selected batch through to all consignees in 3 hours 40 minutes against the organisation's own target of 4 hours. The margin is thin and the scenario used did not involve the Kłaipėda yard, which handles trans-shipment and has different record keeping. **O-02** — The customer excursion notification texts held in Tidewalk differ between the English and German templates in how they describe the hold status; the German text omits the sentence on the 2-hour notification commitment. **O-03** — Firmware release 3.8 for KC-Hub 200 gateways was installed at HAM-2 on 18 July 2024 and at RTM-3 on 25 July 2024 without a recorded change request, contrary to the spirit of clause 6.3 on planning of changes. The organisation is encouraged to formalise IT change records for the food safety scope.

## 8. Certification recommendation and next steps

The lead auditor recommends that certificate FSMS-PL-7741-2021 be **maintained**, subject to the organisation's submission of an acceptable corrective action plan for F-01 by 27 September 2024. Failure to do so will lead to a suspension review under Aldermark rule ACP-11. The recertification audit is planned for 14 to 18 October 2024 (seven audit days, Henrik Solvang-Marchetti as lead). The current certificate expires on 14 December 2024, which leaves a reserve of eight weeks between the end of the recertification audit and the expiry date; certification decisions at Aldermark normally take four to six weeks after the last audit day, so the organisation should not rely on being able to extend the certificate.

## 9. Closing meeting

The closing meeting was held at GDY-1 on 11 September 2024 at 15:30 and lasted 55 minutes. Attendees for Korvane: Marta Kowarzik (CEO), Dieter Ahlbeck-Roe (COO), Benedikta Aaltonen (Quality & Compliance Manager), Jarek Lindqvist (Head of Operations, Gdynia), Gunnar Halvorsrud (Fleet & Maintenance Lead) and Hanneke Dijkgraaf (Depot Manager, Rotterdam; by video link). For Aldermark: Henrik Solvang-Marchetti, Katarzyna Ostrowicz-Bell and Dmitri Fenwick. The organisation did not dispute any finding. Mr Ahlbeck-Roe asked whether F-01 could be downgraded in view of the corrective steps then under discussion, and the lead auditor explained that grading reflects the situation at the time of the audit. Aldermark's complaints and appeals procedure, ACP-19, was handed over in printed form.

*Signed on behalf of Aldermark Certification A/S, Henrik Solvang-Marchetti, lead auditor, 24 September 2024. This report is confidential and is addressed only to the client; copies to third parties require the written consent of both parties. Aldermark's liability is limited as set out in clause 14 of the certification agreement.*
